{
 "date": "2026-11-02",
 "what": "Probe, Kitten and Kokoro against a simulated slower CPU, plus the Kokoro discrepancy measurements",
 "honesty": [
  "Emulation.setCPUThrottlingRate was applied as requested, but Chromium refuses it for workers (\"Operation is only supported for pages, not workers\"). The probe and both engines run in workers, so it changes neither: see cdpThrottleOnly. It is kept only as a negative result.",
  "The slowdown actually used is CPU contention: the run is pinned to 4 logical CPUs (taskset) and k busy-loop processes are pinned to each of them, so each thread gets 1/(k+1) of a core. The nominal factor (1,2,4,6,8,12,20) is k+1. The measured probe ratio confirms it (2.0, 4.0, 6.2, 7.9, 12.2, 21.6).",
  "It slows compute only. It does not slow memory bandwidth, add cache pressure or model a big.LITTLE phone. With 4 ORT threads, contention also stalls threads that wait for each other, so the engine factor falls faster than the single-threaded probe rises; the second sweep with 1 engine thread avoids that.",
  "The host (Ryzen 7 5800XT) was shared and busy (load average about 12 on 16 CPUs). Probe spread over 5 runs was 2.6 to 10 percent.",
  "Test text: \"The capital of France is Paris. This is a short test of on device speech.\" (7.91 s with Kitten, 5.03 s with Kokoro: the same text as the lane results). Warm run = second speak() on an engine that is loaded."
 ],
